We computed the vibrational density of states and infrared spectrum of RDX from 20 ps -long MD trajectories at 300 K from the Fourier transformation of the velocity \({ }^{50}\) and dipole \({ }^{51}\) autocorrelation functions, respectively. Figure 8 compares both quantities between the four parameterizations of ReaxFF and Gen1.9 NNRF and experimental results from refs. \({ }^{52}\) and \({ }^{53}\). Experiments show major infrared active peaks around 1200, 1600, and \(3100 \mathrm{~cm}^{-1}\), corresponding to \(\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) symmetric, \(\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) antisymmetric, and C-H antisymmetric vibrational modes, respectively \({ }^{52-54}\). Interestingly, NNRF matches all three features more closely than ReaxFF. For example, all four versions of ReaxFF
overestimate the frequencies of the hydrogenic modes at \(3100 \mathrm{~cm}^{-1}\).

We now focus on the decomposition and reaction of RDX. Figure 9 tracks key intermediates and final products during an isothermal decomposition simulation at 2000 and 2500 K . Gen 1.9 NNRF at 2500 K predicts \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) to be the most abundant final product, followed by similar amounts of \(\mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), and trace amounts of \(\mathrm{HNCO}, \mathrm{NH}_{3}\), and \(\mathrm{NH}_{4}\); at 2000 K the populations appear to be evolving towards similar proportions. This matches bomb calorimetry experimental observations \({ }^{55}\) where products of detonation were approximately broken down into \(37 \% \mathrm{~N}_{2}, 31 \% \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}, 18 \% \mathrm{CO}_{2}\), and \(14 \% \mathrm{CO}\). The lack on CO is our predictions can be attributed to PBE-D2 underestimating its stability, see Fig. 4. ReaxFF-2014 and ReaxFF-2018 predicted \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) as the dominant product followed by \(\mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). On the other hand, ReaxFF-Ig and ReaxFF-iw predicted comparable amounts of \(\mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) with less \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). Consistent with NNRF ReaxFF does not predict appreciable amounts of CO which was observed experimentally \({ }^{55}\). It is worth mentioning that NNRF predicts \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) and NO as the key intermediates. Using B3LYP flavor of DFT, Chakraborty et al. \({ }^{35}\) calculated, in the gas phase, that the first bond-breaking event should be \(\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\), as compared with HONO elimination and concerted ring break, because it has the lowest dissociation energy. NNRF is consistent with this picture. As an aside, the amount of HONO and \(\mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{CNHNO}_{2}\) was negligible so both species are not shown in Fig. 9. The presence of \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) is consistent with the ReaxFF-Ig and ReaxFF-iw results. However, the ReaxFF-2014 and

